# Delivery Routes

In this notebook you find a short delivery route. A van leaves the depot,
visits four stops once each, and returns. Each candidate is an ordering of the
stops. Genetic search minimizes the round-trip distance with operators that
never drop or repeat a stop, and a parent selector that you supply. The
notebook runs offline and needs no credentials.

## Setup

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:tune, "~> 0.5.0"}
])
```

## Measure a route

Stops sit on a street grid, so the distance between two points is the number
of blocks across plus the number of blocks up or down.

```elixir
stops = %{depot: {0, 0}, a: {0, 2}, b: {2, 2}, c: {2, 0}, d: {1, 0}}

distance = fn route ->
  [:depot | route ++ [:depot]]
  |> Enum.chunk_every(2, 1, :discard)
  |> Enum.sum_by(fn [from, to] ->
    {x, y} = stops[from]
    {u, v} = stops[to]
    abs(x - u) + abs(y - v)
  end)
end

routes = [[:a, :c, :b, :d], [:b, :a, :d, :c], [:d, :b, :a, :c], [:c, :d, :a, :b]]
Enum.map(routes, &{&1, distance.(&1)})
```

```text
[
  {[:a, :c, :b, :d], 12},
  {[:b, :a, :d, :c], 12},
  {[:d, :b, :a, :c], 12},
  {[:c, :d, :a, :b], 12}
]
```

The four starting routes all cover 12 blocks.

## Write permutation-safe operators

A route is a permutation: every stop appears exactly once. Ordinary operators
can break that rule. Joining the front of one route to the back of another can
repeat a stop and lose another.

This crossover keeps a prefix of the left parent, then appends the right
parent's remaining stops in their order. This mutation swaps two positions.
Both take Tune's random state last and return `{new_route, new_random_state}`.

```elixir
crossover = fn left, right, rng ->
  {cut, rng} = :rand.uniform_s(3, rng)
  prefix = Enum.take(left, cut)
  {prefix ++ (right -- prefix), rng}
end

mutate = fn route, rng ->
  {i, rng} = :rand.uniform_s(length(route), rng)
  {j, rng} = :rand.uniform_s(length(route), rng)
  first = Enum.at(route, i - 1)
  second = Enum.at(route, j - 1)
  {route |> List.replace_at(i - 1, second) |> List.replace_at(j - 1, first), rng}
end

rng = :rand.seed_s(:exsss, 3)
{child, rng} = crossover.([:a, :c, :b, :d], [:d, :b, :a, :c], rng)
{swapped, _rng} = mutate.([:a, :b, :c, :d], rng)
{child, swapped}
```

```text
{[:a, :d, :b, :c], [:a, :d, :c, :b]}
```

The child keeps `:a` from the left parent, then takes `:d`, `:b`, and `:c` in
the right parent's order.

## Choose parents with your own policy

By default, genetic search picks each parent by tournament. Pass
`select_parent` to apply your own policy. Tune calls it with the eligible
candidates of the current generation and its random state. It must return
`{candidate_id, new_random_state}`.

This selector always picks the candidate with the best score:

```elixir
strongest = fn candidates, rng ->
  {Enum.max_by(candidates, & &1.evaluation.selected_score).id, rng}
end
```

`selected_score` is always higher-is-better. For a score with
`direction: :minimize`, Tune stores the negated value, so the shortest route
has the largest `selected_score`.

## Run the search

- `direction: :minimize` makes a shorter route a better route.
- `valid?` rejects any child that is not a permutation of the four stops.
- `target_score: 8` is in the score's own units: stop at 8 blocks or fewer.

```elixir
study =
  Tune.new(hd(routes), run: fn route, :roundtrip -> distance.(route) end)
  |> Tune.data(validation: [:roundtrip])
  |> Tune.score(:distance, & &1, direction: :minimize)
  |> Tune.search(:genetic,
    initial_population: routes,
    generations: 12,
    valid?: &(Enum.sort(&1) == [:a, :b, :c, :d]),
    select_parent: strongest,
    crossover_rate: 1,
    mutation_rate: 1,
    crossover: crossover,
    mutate: mutate,
    target_score: 8
  )
  |> Tune.limit(evaluations: 52, seed: 7)

{:ok, winner} = Tune.tune(study)

IO.puts("Selected #{inspect(winner.value)}")
IO.puts("Round-trip distance #{winner.best.evaluation.summaries.distance.mean}")
IO.puts("Stop reason #{winner.stop_reason} after #{length(winner.history)} evaluations")
```

```text
Selected [:d, :c, :b, :a]
Round-trip distance 8.0
Stop reason target_score after 8 evaluations
```

The winner goes depot, `:d`, `:c`, `:b`, `:a`, depot: 1 + 1 + 2 + 2 + 2 = 8
blocks, the perimeter of the grid.

Call the selector on generation zero to see what it receives and returns:

```elixir
generation_zero = Enum.filter(winner.history, &(&1.lineage.generation == 0))

IO.inspect(Enum.map(generation_zero, &{&1.id, &1.evaluation.selected_score}))
{id, _rng} = strongest.(generation_zero, :rand.seed_s(:exsss, 1))
id
```

```text
[{"0", -12.0}, {"1", -12.0}, {"2", -12.0}, {"3", -12.0}]
"0"
```

All four routes tie, so `Enum.max_by/2` returns the first one. Every candidate
in generation 1 comes from route `"0"`, and the swap mutation supplies the
variety. A different policy, such as breaking ties at random, changes which
routes breed.

## Compare with the starting route

`Tune.compare/3` runs the starting route and the winner on the same cases.

```elixir
{:ok, comparison} = Tune.compare(study, winner, split: :validation)
metric = comparison.metrics.distance

IO.puts("#{metric.baseline_mean} -> #{metric.challenger_mean} blocks (#{metric.status}, #{metric.delta})")
```

```text
12.0 -> 8.0 blocks (improved, -4.0)
```

For a minimized score, a negative delta is an improvement.

## Check every route is a permutation

Confirm that no candidate in the history lost or repeated a stop.

```elixir
Enum.all?(winner.history, &(Enum.sort(&1.value) == [:a, :b, :c, :d]))
```

```text
true
```

